Maximilian228's Qwen3 125B Squeeze Runs on a 12 GB Consumer GPU
=E2=96=B2
3,617 Likes
A
125-billion parameter AI model
just got squeezed onto a regular gaming GPU. Here is how it works.
Normally a model this size needs a server rack. A community contributor fig=
ured out how to split the workload:
your GPU handles active computation
, while the bulk of model weights sit in regular system RAM and get pulled =
in as needed. Think of it like streaming from RAM instead of loading everyt=
hing at once.
The model is a
Mixture-of-Experts
(MoE), meaning it only activates a small slice of its 125B parameters per r=
esponse. That is what makes the RAM split feasible.
Minimum setup: 12 GB VRAM + 64 GB RAM, Windows only, NVIDIA driver 580+
Decodes at
177 tokens per second
with 93.6% accuracy vs. the reference model
More VRAM unlocks longer context, up to 262K tokens on a 32 GB card
Only runs on Strata, not llama.cpp, vLLM, or Transformers
The tradeoff is real: you are locked into one Windows-only runtime. But run=
ning a 125B model on desktop hardware opens up local AI at a scale that was=
 not possible before.
