<p style=3D"margin:0 0 15px 0;">NVIDIA just dropped <b>Nemotron Parse 2.0</=
b>, a small but capable model that turns scanned pages and PDFs into clean,=
 structured data you can actually use.</p><p style=3D"margin:0 0 15px 0;">H=
ere's the deal: you feed it a page image, it spits back text with labels, c=
oordinates, and reading order. Think of it like a super-powered scanner tha=
t also understands layout.</p><p style=3D"margin:0 0 15px 0;">What's new in=
 v2.0:</p><ul><li>Charts are now supported, it can convert chart visuals in=
to tables</li><li>Handwriting recognition improved dramatically (error rate=
 dropped from 0.97 to 0.34)</li><li>Indic script accuracy jumped from 6% to=
 72%</li><li>Multilingual OCR score went from 44% to 91%</li></ul><p style=
=3D"margin:0 0 15px 0;">The model runs on a <b>single A100 GPU</b> and uses=
 under 2GB of memory. You load it via Hugging Face Transformers, point it a=
t a PNG of your page, and get back Markdown, JSON, CSV, or HTML.</p><p styl=
e=3D"margin:0;">This opens up local document pipelines with no per-page API=
 costs. Perfect for building search indexes, feeding RAG systems, or extrac=
ting tables into databases. Weights are <b>commercially licensed</b> under =
OpenMDW 1.1.</p>
</div>
