<p style=3D"margin:0 0 15px 0;">A <b>125-billion parameter AI model</b> jus=
t got squeezed onto a regular gaming GPU. Here is how it works.</p><p style=
=3D"margin:0 0 15px 0;">Normally a model this size needs a server rack. A c=
ommunity contributor figured out how to split the workload: <b>your GPU han=
dles active computation</b>, while the bulk of model weights sit in regular=
 system RAM and get pulled in as needed. Think of it like streaming from RA=
M instead of loading everything at once.</p><p style=3D"margin:0 0 15px 0;"=
>The model is a <b>Mixture-of-Experts</b> (MoE), meaning it only activates =
a small slice of its 125B parameters per response. That is what makes the R=
AM split feasible.</p><ul><li>Minimum setup: 12 GB VRAM + 64 GB RAM, Window=
s only, NVIDIA driver 580+</li><li>Decodes at <b>177 tokens per second</b> =
with 93.6% accuracy vs. the reference model</li><li>More VRAM unlocks longe=
r context, up to 262K tokens on a 32 GB card</li><li>Only runs on Strata, n=
ot llama.cpp, vLLM, or Transformers</li></ul><p style=3D"margin:0;">The tra=
deoff is real: you are locked into one Windows-only runtime. But running a =
125B model on desktop hardware opens up local AI at a scale that was not po=
ssible before.</p>
</div>
